[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Why play the Queen's Gambit Declined? It is the most solid answer to 2.c4. Black keeps a pawn on d5, develops quickly and castles early, and White has few ways to start an early attack. Nearly every game of the 1927 world championship match between José Raúl Capablanca and Alexander Alekhine began with it, and it is still a main defense at the top.

The price is the c8 bishop, shut in behind the pawn on e6. Much of Black's play is about freeing that bishop or trading it.

Our setup is the Tartakower Variation, also called the Tartakower–Makogonov–Bondarevsky system, or TMB: ...Nf6, ...Be7, castle, ...h6, then ...b6 and ...Bb7. The fianchetto gives the c8 bishop a long diagonal, and ...b6 supports the freeing break ...c5. It was a regular guest in the world championship matches between Anatoly Karpov and Garry Kasparov.

Compare the other replies to 2.c4. 2...dxc4, the Accepted, gives White the center. 2...c6, the Slav, keeps the bishop free but takes c6 away from the knight, and it needs more theory.

Step through the moves to see the idea.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] [%csl Yc8] Black holds d5 with a pawn. If White takes on d5, Black takes back with the e-pawn and keeps a pawn in the center.

White plays 3.Nc3 in six games out of ten and 3.Nf3 in about one in five.}
3. Nc3 Nf6 {Guards d5 again and prepares to castle. Now White chooses between 4.Bg5 (about a third of games), 4.Nf3 (a quarter) and the Exchange, 4.cxd5 (about one in six).}
4. Bg5 {[%cal Rg5f6] White pins the knight that guards d5.} Be7 {Breaks the pin, so the knight can move again, and prepares to castle.}
5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 {5.Nf3 O-O 6.e3 reaches the same position.} h6 {[%cal Rh6g5] Puts the question to the bishop. 7.Bxf6 Bxf6 gives Black the bishop pair, and after 7.Bh4 Black can play ...b6 without worrying about the pin. The pawn on h6 also gives Black's king an escape square later.}
7. Bh4 b6 {[%cal Gc8b7,Gc7c5] The Tartakower. Next comes ...Bb7, and later ...c5 to free the game. The plans are on the next page.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "rnbq1rk1/p1p1bpp1/1p2pn1p/3p4/2PP3B/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 0 8"]

{[%cal Gc8b7,Gc7c5,Gf6e4,Gb8d7,Rc4d5,Ra1c1,Rf1d3] [%csl Yd5] The Tartakower position after 7...b6. The green arrows are Black's plans, the red arrows White's. Stockfish rates it as equal (+0.1).

Black's plan. ...Bb7 puts the bishop on the long diagonal, behind the pawn on d5. ...Nbd7 develops the last minor piece, and ...c5 hits White's center. When White's pieces allow it, ...Ne4 trades pieces and frees Black's game.

White's main tries, and our answers:

8.cxd5, four games in ten: take back with the knight, 8...Nxd5. The trades 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5 free Black's game, and ...Be6 and ...c5 give Black active hanging pawns (next page).

8.Bd3, about one game in four: 8...Bb7 9.O-O Nbd7 10.Qe2 c5. Against 11.Rfd1 or 11.Rad1, 11...Ne4 trades pieces (Stockfish +0.1, equal).

8.Be2, 8.Rc1, 8.Qc2 and 8.Qb3: 8...Bb7 and ...Nbd7, then ...c5, or ...dxc4 and ...c5 once White's bishop has moved. Stockfish rates all of them as equal.

7.Bxf6 instead of 7.Bh4: take back with the bishop. Then ...c6, ...Nd7 and ...dxc4 prepare ...e5 or ...c5, and Stockfish rates the position as equal.

The Exchange (4.cxd5 exd5) fixes the pawn structure, and the plans are different. See Meeting the Exchange.

4.Nf3 Be7 5.Bf4: castle, then 6.e3 c5 7.dxc5 Bxc5 frees the game (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).

The Catalan (3.Nf3 Nf6 4.g3): ...Be7 and castle, then take on c4 once White has castled: 6...dxc4 7.Qc2 a6 8.Qxc4 b5 9.Qc2 Bb7 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "rn3rk1/p3qpp1/4b2p/2pp4/8/3BPN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R w K - 0 14"]

{[%cal Gd5d4,Gc5c4,Ge6f5,Rc1c5,Rd1d5] [%csl Yc5,Yd5] The hanging pawns. This is the main line after 8.cxd5 Nxd5 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5 11.Rc1 Be6 12.Bd3 c5 13.dxc5 bxc5. Stockfish rates it as equal (0.0).

Black's pawns on c5 and d5 stand side by side, with no pawns next to them, so pieces must guard them. In return they control b4, c4, d4 and e4, and they can advance.

Keep them side by side. If one pawn is forced forward, the other can become weak. Push only with a purpose: ...d4 opens lines when White's pieces are not ready, and ...c4 gains space when it drives White's bishop back. For example, 13.O-O instead of 13.dxc5 allows 13...c4! 14.Bb1, and Black's queenside pawns have more room (Stockfish 0.0, equal).

Bishop on e6, not b7. Once the d5 pawn is fixed, a bishop on b7 bites on its own pawn. On e6 it guards d5 and can come to f5 or g4.

Take back with the pawn. After 13.dxc5, recapture 13...bxc5. A piece on c5 would leave Black with an isolated pawn on d5 and give White the d4 square, and here 13...Qxc5?? even loses the queen to 14.Rxc5 (see Tricks and traps).

Develop around them: ...Nd7, then the knight to f6 or b6, the rooks to c8 and b8, and ...a5.

The Carlsbad structure, after the Exchange: Black's pawns on c6 and d5 face White's on d4 and e3, and Black has the half-open e-file. See Meeting the Exchange.

The isolated pawn. If Black ever recaptures on c5 with a piece, the pawn on d5 is isolated. Then Black needs active pieces, and White aims to blockade d4 and trade pieces.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/5n2/3N2B1/3P4/8/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 0 6"]

{[%cal Gf6d5,Gf8b4,Rg5d8] Five patterns to know.

The Elephant trap. In the Exchange, after 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7, the pawn on d5 looks free, because the knight on f6 is pinned. White grabs it with 6.Nxd5?? in about one game in five, and this is the position. Play 6...Nxd5! White can take your queen with 7.Bxd8, but 7...Bb4+ forces 8.Qd2 Bxd2+ 9.Kxd2 Kxd8, and you are a piece up (Stockfish −5.1, winning for Black). The trap works only with the knight on d7, which is one reason we play 5...Nbd7.

Take back with the bishop. After 6...h6 7.Bxf6, recapture 7...Bxf6. 7...gxf6? wrecks the pawns in front of your king, and White's pieces aim at it with Bd3 and Nh4 (Stockfish +3.0, winning for White).

Take back with the knight. Against 8.cxd5, play 8...Nxd5. The tempting 8...c5? fails to 9.dxc6, and White has won a pawn (Stockfish +1.8, clearly better for White).

Take back with the pawn. In the hanging-pawn line, after 11.Rc1 Be6 12.Bd3 c5 13.dxc5, recapture 13...bxc5. 13...Qxc5?? loses the queen to 14.Rxc5, because the rook on c1 attacks it (Stockfish +7.6, winning for White). Check which pieces see c5 before you recapture there.

Wait for the bishop. Take on c4 only after White's bishop has left f1. Then Bxc4 costs White a second move with the same bishop, and you gain a tempo. In the Tartakower, after 8.Bd3, 8...dxc4 9.Bxc4 is fine for Black (Stockfish +0.1, equal); against 8.Rc1 or 8.Qc2, wait.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bqrnk1/pp2bppp/2p2n2/3p2B1/3P4/2NBP3/PPQ1NPPP/R4RK1 w - - 8 11"]

{[%cal Gf6e4,Gf8g6,Ge7d6,Gc8e6,Ra1b1,Rb2b4,Rb4b5,Rf2f3] [%csl Yc6] The Exchange Variation from Black's side, after 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7 6.e3 c6 7.Bd3 Be7 8.Qc2 O-O 9.Nge2 Re8 10.O-O Nf8. The green arrows are Black's plans, the red arrows White's. Stockfish rates it as equal (+0.2).

The trade on d5 fixed the pawns: White's d4 and e3 against Black's d5 and c6. It also freed your c8 bishop and gave you the half-open e-file.

Your setup: ...c6 to hold d5, ...Be7, castle, ...Re8, then ...Nf8. The knight on f8 guards h7 and goes on to g6 or e6.

Against the minority attack (Rb1, b4, b5): don't open lines for White. ...a6 makes White spend a move on a4 before b5. Meanwhile play on the kingside, where you have more space: ...Ne4 trades pieces, and ...Ng6 and ...Bd6 aim at h2.

Against f3 and e4: White wants to open the center. Get ready with ...g6 and ...Ne6, which hits d4 and g5.

The c8 bishop: trade it with ...Be6 or ...Bg4 when White allows it, or play ...g6 and ...Bf5. While White's queen and bishop control f5, it often stays on d7 or e6.

If White plays Nf3 instead of Nge2, there is no f3 plan, and White usually goes for the minority attack. Then ...Ng6 and ...Ne4 are your counterplay (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White, after 9.Nf3 Re8 10.O-O Nf8).} *
